tion":0.007724961169891357,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":87.1,"_gate_insider_own":0.00727,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":4.8,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":["paper"],"description_full":"H&R Block, Inc., through its subsidiaries, provides assisted and do-it-yourself (DIY) tax preparation solutions in the United States, Canada, and Australia. The company offers tax preparation services, such as assisted income tax return preparation and related services through a system of retail offices operated directly or through its franchisees in person or virtually; refund transfers for clients to receive tax refunds; Peace of Mind, an extended service plan; and H&R Block Emerald Prepaid Mastercard and Spruce debit cards. It also provides H&R Block Emerald Advance, a term loan; Tax Identity Shield, a program for protection of tax identity and access to services to help restore tax identity; refund advance loans; H&R Block's Instant Refund, an advance refund; and small business financial solutions, such as payment processing, payroll and bookkeeping services. H&R Block, Inc. was incorporated in 1955 and is headquartered in Kansas City, Missouri.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Curtis A. Campbell | CEO, President & Director | Age 52 | Compensation: $1.9M (FY2026)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 4K people. Revenue per employee of $858K ranks in the top quartile among Consumer Cyclical peers. Each employee generates $164K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $7K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 2\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["H&R Block, Inc. is a mid-cap consumer cyclical business with a $5.6B market cap on $3.9B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +4% over the past three years and +3% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 118% against a 7% cost of capital \u2014 a 111-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $756M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 19 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.14\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.5\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage runs at 11.6\u00d7.","Shareholder yield of 3.8% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 3.0\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Very elevated short interest (18.9% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"News sentiment running negative (-0.12) across recent coverage","sev":"amber","cat":"news"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Consumer Cyclical tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Consumer staples outperforming discretionary, signaling cautious spending","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"High market volatility with VIX at 36, signaling elevated fear","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Widening credit spreads suggest growing stress in corporate debt markets","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Broad market in a downtrend, trading below its 200-day average","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Multiple macro indicators signal economic contraction","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Brand relevance decay \u2014 shifting 